---
// About Live Earth. The title, headings, order and step format follow the about-page skill
// (.agents/skills/about-page/SKILL.md): plain words that are the same on every lab. Counts
// come from src/lib/views.ts at build time.
import LabLayout from 'kp-design/layouts/LabLayout.astro';
import '../../styles/theme.css';
import { SATELLITES } from '../../lib/views';
import { formatLon } from '../../lib/sun';

const viewCount = SATELLITES.reduce((n, s) => n + s.views.length, 0);
// Some regions (the Pacific Northwest, for one) come from both satellites; count them once.
const regionCount = new Set(SATELLITES.flatMap((s) => s.views.filter((v) => v.group === 'region').map((v) => v.id))).size;
const [east, west, meteosat] = ['east', 'west', 'meteosat'].map((id) => SATELLITES.find((s) => s.id === id)!);
const year = new Date().getUTCFullYear();

const steps = [
  {
    name: 'The satellites scan the planet',
    where: 'GOES-19, GOES-18 and Meteosat-12, 35,786 km above the equator',
    body: [
      `Three weather satellites stay over the same spot on the equator as the Earth turns: NOAA’s ${west.name} (${west.craft}) at ${formatLon(west.lon)} and ${east.name} (${east.craft}) at ${formatLon(east.lon)}, and EUMETSAT’s ${meteosat.craft} at ${formatLon(meteosat.lon)}, over Africa. Each scans its whole side of the planet every 10 minutes; the GOES pair also scan the United States and smaller regions every 5 or 10.`,
    ],
  },
  {
    name: 'NOAA and EUMETSAT turn each scan into a picture',
    where: 'NOAA’s Center for Satellite Applications and Research (STAR), and EUMETSAT in Darmstadt',
    body: [
      'Both blend the satellite’s channels into a natural-color picture (GeoColor at NOAA, GeoColour at EUMETSAT): true color in daylight, and at night clouds over city lights. NOAA publishes each GOES picture in several sizes, usually 5 to 25 minutes after the scan starts.',
      'EUMETSAT serves Meteosat as a flat map, cut to any area and size on request. Live Earth uses the scan on the hour, once it’s at least 35 minutes old, which keeps it within EUMETSAT’s free data.',
    ],
  },
  {
    name: 'Your browser finds the newest picture',
    where: 'Your phone or computer',
    body: [
      'The page works out when the latest scans should have started and asks for the smallest copy of each, newest first, until one is there. That small copy shows right away while a full-size one loads, picked to match your screen.',
      'Every minute while the page is open, it checks for a newer scan and swaps it in.',
    ],
  },
  {
    name: 'Your browser trims and shows it',
    where: 'Your phone or computer',
    body: [
      'NOAA stamps its logo and a caption bar along the bottom of every picture. The page masks the whole-Earth view to a circle, and trims the bottom edge of the others (for some sizes, a thin strip down the left edge too), so what’s left is the planet.',
      'Meteosat’s whole Earth arrives as a flat map, so the page bends it back into the round planet the satellite sees, pixel by pixel, using the satellite’s viewing geometry. Europe and Africa show as flat maps. Time-lapses load up to 72 smaller frames and play them in order.',
    ],
  },
];
---

<LabLayout
  title="About Live Earth"
  description="What Live Earth is, how it works and what it collects."
  image="/live-earth/og-about.png"
  imageAlt="About Live Earth: what it is and how it works, on kylepace.dev."
  themeColor={{ light: '#f3f5f7', dark: '#05080c' }}
>
  <a slot="bar" class="bar-link" href="/live-earth/">Live Earth</a>

  <article class="lab-page about-page prose">
    <h1 class="title">About Live Earth</h1>

    <section aria-labelledby="what-title">
      <h2 id="what-title">What it is</h2>
      <p>
        <a href="/live-earth/">Live Earth</a> shows the newest picture from three weather satellites, as big as your
        screen allows: the whole planet from over the Pacific, the Americas or Africa, the United States, or one of
        {regionCount} regions ({viewCount} views in all). It’s
        meant for leaving open on a spare screen, and it’s the first test of a wallpaper and widget app built on the same
        pictures.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="use-title">
      <h2 id="use-title">How to use it</h2>
      <p>
        Pick a satellite, then a view. The strip above the satellite buttons is the equator: each satellite sits at its real
        longitude, and the shaded half is the side of the planet in daylight right now, with the sun where it’s noon. The
        satellite closer to the sun is chosen for you when the page opens.
      </p>
      <p>
        Time plays back the last 3 or 12 hours as a loop; drag the slider to step through frames. Detail set to High uses
        NOAA’s largest files, up to about 10 MB a picture, for big or very sharp screens. Full screen hides everything but
        the picture until you move the pointer.
      </p>
      <p>The address bar keeps your satellite and view, so you can bookmark or share a view.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="how-title">
      <h2 id="how-title">How it works</h2>
      <ol class="steps">
        {
          steps.map((step) => (
            <li>
              <h3>{step.name}</h3>
              <p class="where">Where it runs: {step.where}.</p>
              {step.body.map((text) => <p>{text}</p>)}
            </li>
          ))
        }
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="data-title">
      <h2 id="data-title">Where the data comes from</h2>
      <p>
        GOES pictures are GeoColor imagery from NOAA/NESDIS/STAR, loaded straight from
        <a href="https://www.star.nesdis.noaa.gov/GOES/">NOAA’s GOES Image Viewer</a> service. They’re work of the U.S.
        government and in the public domain. GeoColor was developed by CIRA at Colorado State University with NOAA.
      </p>
      <p>
        Meteosat pictures contain modified EUMETSAT Meteosat data {year}: GeoColour imagery from Meteosat-12, loaded from
        <a href="https://view.eumetsat.int/">EUMETView</a> and used under EUMETSAT’s
        <a href="https://www.eumetsat.int/eumetsat-data-licensing">data policy</a> for Core data (CC BY 4.0). The
        whole-Earth view is redrawn in your browser.
      </p>
      <p>Live Earth isn’t made or endorsed by NOAA or EUMETSAT.</p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="good-title">
      <h2 id="good-title">Good to know</h2>
      <p>
        A GOES picture is usually 5 to 25 minutes old, since that’s how long NOAA takes to post a scan. A Meteosat picture
        updates once an hour and is 35 to 95 minutes old, so its time-lapses step an hour at a time. Now and then NOAA skips a
        scan or a satellite goes into maintenance; the page shows the newest one it can find and says how old it is.
      </p>
      <p>
        Together the satellites cover the Pacific, the Americas, the Atlantic, Europe and Africa. Most of Asia and Australia
        are out of view. Meteosat’s Europe sits low on the planet’s curve from the equator, so the far north looks
        stretched. Hiding NOAA’s logo and caption costs a thin slice at the bottom or left of each region and the outermost edge of the planet.
      </p>
      <p>
        Pictures are big: a whole-Earth picture is about 2 MB at Standard detail, and a 12-hour time-lapse loads about 25 MB.
        On a phone plan, Live is the light option.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="privacy-title">
      <h2 id="privacy-title">Privacy</h2>
      <p>
        Analytics works the same as the rest of kylepace.dev: anonymous, no cookies, and you can turn it off (<a
          href="https://kylepace.dev/privacy/">privacy</a
        >). Live Earth records which satellite, view, time-lapse and detail level get picked. Pictures come straight from
        NOAA’s image server (cdn.star.nesdis.noaa.gov) and EUMETSAT’s map service (view.eumetsat.int), which see your
        request like any visit to a website; the page sends no referrer with it. Your detail choice is kept in your browser.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="contact-title">
      <h2 id="contact-title">Get in touch</h2>
      <p>
        Questions or something that looks wrong: email <a href="mailto:mail@kylepace.net">mail@kylepace.net</a>. The code is public:{' '}
        <a href="https://github.com/kyle-pace/live-earth">github.com/kyle-pace/live-earth</a>.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</LabLayout>
